 about 85  to 90 percent of all the passenger  volume the small airports are much more  dependent on federal grants but for the  vast majority of us travelers these  airports could be completely self square  the federal grants could become  irrelevant so our proposal is that we  end all we end federal grants for all  passenger airports including the small  ones because they would just need to  charge higher passenger facility charges  and maybe make their landing fees a  little more realistic remove the limits  that the federal government Congress  imposes limits at the behest of the  airlines on the passenger facility fees  but the trade-off would be you can lower  the the ticket tax on the one side and  on the other side let the airports  self-fund them it's a it's a and I  managed to sell this to Grover Norquist  as a devolution of the federal shrinking  the federal role and this is a sea  change because Grover Norquist's and his  organization at NTU have historically  always bought into the airport airline  the airline's propaganda line that the  federal limit on the passenger facility  charge is actually protecting the people  from a federal tax increase it's  completely backwards but Congress always  falls for that so that we may we may  change at this time and remove the  limits on airport privatization air  traffic control you know I've given  talks on that a reason weekend this is  another thing that it is largely the  dollars coming in in your ticket taxes  pay for most of the costs of running the  air traffic control system but this is  such a mess  of a big bureaucracy that's trying to  run a high-tech service business and is  just very poorly suited to the task so  we need and our proposal which we've  been making in various forums for over  20 years is to separate the air traffic  control operation from the faa faa  should be a safety just a safety  regulator of aviation that's a big  enough job for it to do a shift to  direct user fees for for aircraft  control which would enable the spun off  preferably nonprofit corporation to  issue revenue bonds to pay for the 20  billion dollar modernization that  they're trying to do now out of annual  operating cash flow appropriated by  Congress with Congress making a lot of  priorities is on what they should spend  it on have arms like safety regulations  so forth highways up as I mentioned the  Highway Trust Fund was originally set up  as a pretty good user financing user  funding mechanism for building the  interstate highway system but once it  was the original plan was largely built  out Congress all we had this great  revenue stream what else can we do it  well we can give them grants for their  state highways we can give them grants  for transit that happened under Ronald  Reagan by the way the opening it up for  transit funding so today hundred percent  of money the federal highway gas taxes  comes from automobiles and trucks but  only seventy percent of it actually is  spent on highways the other thirty  percent is all these other non Highway  Transportation stuff mostly urban  transit so we propose rethink the  federal role make it the interstate  routes only which is the actual  interstate highways and some of the  others that are called national highway  system that many of them should be  upgraded to interstates remove the  federal restrictions that are imposed on  states that prevent them from using  tolling so give the state's tools to  cope with the bigger responsibilities of  being responsible for all the rest of  the highway system and and build up  support for the concept of highways as a  network utility that's paid for directly  by their users so for transit what  happens to transit then if the Federals  are feds are no longer providing well  the situation with transit is that a  hundred percent you know when people  ride washington metro and you know buy  their tickets and so forth a hundred  percent of the capital costs of building  transit systems