 has to do big study and Congress every  year will appropriates money but the  amount they appropriate every year is  only about half of what comes in in the  harbor maintenance tax because OMB likes  to hoard that money so that it makes the  federal budget deficit look smaller this  used to happen with  yes and Trust Fund and of the highway  and that Congress got rid of that  eventually but it still happens with  Harbor but the biggest flaw is that  ports all compete with one another all  the West Coast ports compete with one  another the West Coast ports compete  with the Gulf and the East Coast ports  so why should the federal government be  picking winners and losers and why  should the ports that have naturally  deep harbors like Oakland Los Angeles  Seattle Norfolk that don't need dredging  or need very little be paying money for  harbor dredging to help their  competitors and yet they do it because  that's the system as they all figure  well we better we're better off together  in the American Association of Port  Authority's all singing from the same  hymn book than to maybe have some ports  have to go out of business because they  just aren't competitive so our proposal  is to abolish the harbor maintenance tax  and the harbor maintenance trust fund  because ports can naturally self-fund  port of miami is actually doing that now  so they can dredge the 50 feet to meet  the larger ships because they're not  sure they're going to get their federal  money and if they wait it may be 10  years until they get it so they're  issuing bonds and doing it themselves  finally inland waterways you know he has  whole huge network you know on the  Mississippi River the Ohio River and so  forth barge operators pay a user tax on  diesel fuel goes into a trust fund for  maintaining and improving the inland  waterway system user tax only covers  eight percent of the total cost of  operating and maintaining and improving  the waterways the main biggest thing is  what expense the other is that locks and  dams are mostly obsolete and they're not  big enough they're in poor shape and so  forth and again Congress doesn't  appropriate enough money because both of  it has to come out of the general fund  and that is just clearly gone you know  going to be gone very soon we're not  going to have it so our proposal and  this is actually this has actually been  suggested in a report the Army Corps of  Engineers has a little research  institute and they've sort of looked at  the future of this about a year ago and  said well there may we may have to  really change the model here and one of  the alternatives could be to have  individual waterways developed in some  kind of a user pay mekkin  nism that would pay for at least mode  they say most we say all of the costs of  rebuilding the locks and dams and so  forth and and Havelock fees as a  bondable revenue stream but do it  corridor by corridor and each one B  could be kind of a self-governing thing  and there's actually a little bit of  traction for that idea now because  people can the smarter ones that can  look further ahead can see the  handwriting on the wall and say this  this system is not going to be  sustainable in the way we've had it for  the past 40 or 50 years so now one thing  also public-private partnerships you  know we've seen this reason has played a  big role in introducing long-term ppps  for highway projects such as the Capital  Beltway if you've been to Washington  recently they just opened in the fall  this finally express toll lanes on a  major portion of the Capital Beltway  around Washington DC it's a huge huge  improvement it's done by a 70-year toll  concession entirely at risk by the  private sector if if neither of people  use them and not willing to pay the  rates that they need to do the variable  toll rates to keep traffic flowing at 50  60 miles an hour then they're at risk  and there's no taxpayer the taxpayers  are not at risk in this so there's a  whole lot of things we can do with this  model applied not only highways express  lane networks airport terminals  LaGuardia Airport there they're planning  to do this for the new Central Terminal  the RFP is going to be out in a few  months we can pay for air traffic  control modernization this way dredging  harbors and channels new terminals at  ports there's a lot of opportunities for  using this model it's very well-suited  once you have financing and once you  have true user fees in place now some  people can say well the government can  do all this airports are ready efforts  are all government-owned they're already  doing this that's true so what value  does and we have toll road agencies in  many states that about a third of the  states have toward what is the private  sector add to what you could do with a  government authority well risk transfer  is a big one because taxpayers are  ultimately backstopping the government  airport authorities and toll agencies in  the long-term properly structured PPP  only the investors are at risk for that  and that's a big difference megaprojects  you know billion dollar scale projects  have a very bad track record you the big  dig in Boston anybody remember of big  cost overruns late completion /  optimistic forecasts of how many people  are using how much we will pay for it  and so forth so risk scratcher is a huge  advantage of ppps guaranteed long-term  maintenance the people who finance these  projects the first thing they look at is  because you're expecting to attract  people to pay user fees is it going to  be is there a guarantee that it's going  to be properly maintained for the entire  lifetime of the thing and so that is  that is true in these kinds of  arrangements so you have to guarantee  you have to prove to the investors that  they'll be properly maintained for the  entire period out of the user fee  revenues in order to get the money in  the first place now the additional  investment we have globally over the  last